In the model:

  for (k in 1:K) log_theta[k] = log(theta[k]);
  lp = rep_vector(-log(K), K); ## Initial state log-probability
  for (n in 1:N) {
    for (k in 1:K)
      lp[k]
        = log_sum_exp(log_theta[k] + lp)

                                   ^^^^^

        + wrappedCauchy(phi[n], rho[k], mu[k])
        + exponential_lpdf(l[n] | alpha[k]);
  }
  target += log_sum_exp(lp);

the part highlighted with ^^^^ isn't correct.

Thinking in terms of a N x K matrix for lp, what you want in
general for an HMM is this:

   lp[n + 1, k] = SUM_k' lp[n, k']                     // previous state density
                         + log_theta[k', k]            // transition probability
                         + log p_emission(y[n] | theta(k)) // emission density

...

   target += log_sum_exp(lp[N]);   // final column for last time N

You can do this with two lp vectors in a rolling fashion, but you
can't do it with just one.

Your y[n] = (phi[n], l[n]) and your theta[k] = (rho[k], mu[k], alpha[k])
and the p_emission

- Bob

