Ah, the case of unequal numbers of replicates is one I've been meaning to tackle. I'll work up an example once I get on a proper machine (on my tablet right now), but using the variable names from the equal-numbers-of-replicates example, what you'd do is have as data: (1) vector of unique X values, (2) vector of all Y values, (3) vector of X-values associated with each Y ("X_val"). Then, in transformed data, compute the distance matrix between each unique X value, and finally the only change to my example is that instead of

  for(m in 1:M){
    Y[m] ~ normal(f,noise) ;
  }

you would have

  for(ny in 1:nY){ #nY is the total number of observations
    Y[ny] ~ normal( f[X_val[ny]] , noise ) ;
  }

which I think you can un-loop as:

  Y ~ normal( f[X_val] , noise) ;

