MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I set it up that way originally because it seemed natural
to put any transforms together in one block and save the
transformed parameters, then put all the density statements
in the model block.  Just seemed like the natural organization
for programming a model.

We have toyed with two competing ideas:

1.  eliminating transformed parameters and saving variables
    at top of model block, and

2.  adding jacobian += statements to the transformed parameters
    block that would add to the Jacobian for the transformed (it
    is controlled by the algorithms---the Jacobian is turned off
    for optimization)

- Bob

> On Sep 8, 2016, at 1:08 AM, Daniel Lee <bea...@alum.mit.edu> wrote:
>
> Hi Mike,
>
> On Wed, Sep 7, 2016 at 10:39 PM, Mike Lawrence <mike....@gmail.com> wrote:
> Hi folks,
>
> Possibly a quick'un. Is there any reason that variables declared in the model block are not saved?
>
> I'll answer it quickly: yes. I don't know off the top of my head, but there are reasons. I know how it's implemented and it's not impossible to change it, but it's not easy.
>
> For more info, look in the Manual v2.12.0: section 24.7, table 29.1, section 29.2, figure 29.2 ("print" refers to output).
>
>
> Take this example of a 1D Gaussian Process model
>
> data {
>     // nX: num unique X values
>     int<lower=1> nX ;
>     // X: unique x values
>     vector[nX] X ;
>     // nY: number of observations total
>     int<lower=1> nY ;
>     // Y: data
>     vector[nY] Y ;
>     // XY: *index* of x value per observation in Y
>     int XY[nY] ;
>     // fudge: a fudge factor for computation
>     real<lower=0> fudge ;
>   }
>   transformed data {
>     // dx: computing the pairwise distances between x values
>     matrix[nX,nX] dx ;
>     //off-diagonal
>     for (i in 1:(nX-1)) {
>       for (j in (i+1):nX) {
>         dx[i,j] = -( X[i] - X[j] )^2  ;
>         dx[j,i] = dx[i,j] ; // lower mirrors upper
>       }
>     }
>     //diagonal
>     for (nx in 1:nX){
>       dx[nx,nx] = 0 ;
>     }
>   }
>   parameters {
>     // noise: sampling noise for replicates
>     real<lower=0> noise ;
>     // eta_intercept: GP parameter, scale of output (Y)
>     real<lower=0> eta ;
>     // rho_intercept: GP parameter, x-axis scale of influence between points (wiggliness)
>     real<lower=0> rho ;
>     // z: dummy variable for quicker multivariate normal computation for GP
>     vector[nX] z ;
>   }
>   model {
>     // f: function values
>     vector[nX] f ;
>     // priors on GP kernel parameters
>     eta ~ weibull(2,1) ;
>     rho ~ student_t(4,0,1) ;
>     // prior on noise
>     noise ~ weibull(2,1) ;
>     z ~ normal(0,1) ;
>     {
>       // L: cholesky decomposition
>       matrix[nX,nX] L ;
>       // covMat: covariance matrix
>       matrix[nX,nX] covMat ;
>       //  covMat off-diagonal
>       for (i in 1:(nX-1)) {
>         for (j in (i+1):nX) {
>           covMat[i,j] = eta * exp(dx[i,j]*rho) ;
>           covMat[j,i] = covMat[i,j] ; // lower mirrors upper
>         }
>       }
>       // covMat diagonal: adding jitter to avoid computation issues ??
>       for (nx in 1:nX){
>         covMat[nx,nx] = eta + fudge ;
>       }
>       L = cholesky_decompose(covMat) ;
>       // assert sampling of f as zero-centered GP
>       f = L * z ;
>     }
>     // assert sampling of each replicate of Y as GP plus noise
>     Y ~ normal(f[XY],noise) ;
>   }
>
> This runs fine, but the variable "f" isn't in the rstan::stanfit object. I know the variables declared in the local block within the model block (ex. L & covMat) won't be saved for access in the stanfit object, but I would have thought "f" would be. I know the solution is to declare f in the transformed parameters block, and cut-and-paste the entire local block currently (in the above code) in the model block to the transformed parameters block, yielding:
>
>   data {
>     // nX: num unique X values
>     int<lower=1> nX ;
>     // X: unique x values
>     vector[nX] X ;
>     // nY: number of observations total
>     int<lower=1> nY ;
>     // Y: data
>     vector[nY] Y ;
>     // XY: *index* of x value per observation in Y
>     int XY[nY] ;
>     // fudge: a fudge factor for computation
>     real<lower=0> fudge ;
>   }
>   transformed data {
>     // dx: computing the pairwise distances between x values
>     matrix[nX,nX] dx ;
>     //off-diagonal
>     for (i in 1:(nX-1)) {
>       for (j in (i+1):nX) {
>         dx[i,j] = -( X[i] - X[j] )^2  ;
>         dx[j,i] = dx[i,j] ; // lower mirrors upper
>       }
>     }
>     //diagonal
>     for (nx in 1:nX){
>       dx[nx,nx] = 0 ;
>     }
>   }
>   parameters {
>     // noise: sampling noise for replicates
>     real<lower=0> noise ;
>     // eta_intercept: GP parameter, scale of output (Y)
>     real<lower=0> eta ;
>     // rho_intercept: GP parameter, x-axis scale of influence between points (wiggliness)
>     real<lower=0> rho ;
>     // z: dummy variable for quicker multivariate normal computation for GP
>     vector[nX] z ;
>   }
>   transformed parameters{
>     // f: function values
>     vector[nX] f ;
>     {
>       // L: cholesky decomposition
>       matrix[nX,nX] L ;
>       // covMat: covariance matrix
>       matrix[nX,nX] covMat ;
>       //  covMat off-diagonal
>       for (i in 1:(nX-1)) {
>         for (j in (i+1):nX) {
>           covMat[i,j] = eta * exp(dx[i,j]*rho) ;
>           covMat[j,i] = covMat[i,j] ; // lower mirrors upper
>         }
>       }
>       // covMat diagonal: adding jitter to avoid computation issues ??
>       for (nx in 1:nX){
>         covMat[nx,nx] = eta + fudge ;
>       }
>       L = cholesky_decompose(covMat) ;
>       // assert sampling of f as zero-centered GP
>       f = L * z ;
>     }
>   }
>   model {
>     // priors on GP kernel parameters
>     eta ~ weibull(2,1) ;
>     rho ~ student_t(4,0,1) ;
>     // prior on noise
>     noise ~ weibull(2,1) ;
>     z ~ normal(0,1) ;
>     // assert sampling of each replicate of Y as GP plus noise
>     Y ~ normal(f[XY],noise) ;
>   }
>
> But for teaching purposes I thought it might be more straightforward to omit the transformed parameters block altogether. That said, I just noticed that the version using the transformed parameters block actually computes with better ESS/time; any idea why that might be?
>
> I just scanned it -- it's a bit too long. But if it's just moving a variable declared in the model block over to the transformed parameters block, then you shouldn't see a difference in ESS. There might be some time difference with IO on your system, but if it's not too large, then it shouldn't really make too much of a difference. Identical data? Next step would be to set seeds. And then check that the system is free.
>
>
> Thanks!
>
> Mike
