Hi Linas,

sorry for my late reply - quite busy right now. So yes, you have to take the partial derivatives of the ODE RHS (one Jacobian wrt to the states and another one wrt to the parameters). Your example looks correct to me.

The beauty of using dMod is that it spits out the derivatives for you.

I will dig out the stan model you asked for later.

Sebastian

On Wed, Sep 7, 2016 at 7:55 PM, Linas Mockus <linasm...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Sebastian,
>
> I have some issues because of the nonlinearies. Suppose in two compartmental model flow into central compartment is described as da/dt=-ka*a. When I take element of jacobian for state a I get -ka. In the code you sent (oral_2cmt_jacobian.hpp) it is represented as J[0]=-ka. Now let's introduce nonlinearities and suppose da/dt=-ka*a*c - this is a case for PBPK models I am dealing with. Then my guess would be that  J[0]=-ka should be replaced by J[0]=-ka*y[1] where y[1] represents state c while y[0] is representing state a. Could you please help?
>
> Thank you,
> Linas
