> > > > >
> > > > > I actually was using the same data and seeds, as well as lots of iterations and lots of chains. The models and R code for generating the data are here (https://gist.github.com/mike-lawrence/068c2f1b41490322973fd8fc366ea61b), though you can skip lines 34 through 107 (exploring the "many_reps.stan" model).
> > > > >
> > > > > I'm using a 10-core VM in google's compute engine, so I bumped up all sampling to 10 parallel chains & 2e4 iterations each.
> > > > >
> > > > > Here's the output/timing from the model ("many_unequal_reps.stan") that does computations in the transformed parameters block:
> > > > >
> > > > >         mean se_mean   sd 2.5% 97.5%  n_eff Rhat
> > > > >   noise 1.03       0 0.02 0.99  1.08 100000    1
> > > > >   eta   1.22       0 0.37 0.63  2.06  52560    1
> > > > >   rho   0.27       0 0.09 0.14  0.49  25629    1
> > > > >   > mean(rowSums(get_elapsed_time(post2)))/60
> > > > >   [1] 20.159
> > > > >   > sd(rowSums(get_elapsed_time(post2)))/60
> > > > >   [1] 0.104228
> > > > >
> > > > > And here's the output/timing from the model ("many_unequal_reps2.stan") that *doesn't* do computations in the transformed parameters block:
> > > > >
> > > > >         mean se_mean   sd 2.5% 97.5%  n_eff Rhat
> > > > >   noise 1.03       0 0.02 0.99  1.08 100000    1
> > > > >   eta   1.23       0 0.37 0.64  2.06  48137    1
> > > > >   rho   0.27       0 0.09 0.14  0.49  24539    1
> > > > >   > mean(rowSums(get_elapsed_time(post3)))/60
> > > > >   [1] 29.12736
> > > > >   > sd(rowSums(get_elapsed_time(post3)))/60
> > > > >   [1] 0.3682616
> > > > >
> > > > > Admittedly, 30%ish isn't a huge performance difference, but I was surprised to see any difference this consistent at all.
