Hi Bob,

    Thanks, I tested a multivariate normal prior for intercept and slope. It converges faster and solves the first problem I posted in the topic. As for priors for variances, I set a normal prior in FullGP for tau, but I still use the inverse-Gamma for "sigmasq" so that I don't need to take sigma^2 later. What's more, I think variogram is more widely used in spatial analysis, the mean of the prior for "sigmasq" is actually got from the empirical variogram in the exploration of data analysis ( please check EDA in subfolder "project"). So I think it is also nature to work with "sigmasq."

In fullGP.stan, I changed my code into:

...
...

parameters {

    vector[2] beta;

    real<lower = 0> sigmasq;

    real<lower = 0> tausq;

    real<lower = ap, upper = bp> phi;

}

model{

    beta ~ multi_normal(uB, VB);

    sigmasq ~ inv_gamma(as, bs);

    tausq ~ inv_gamma(at, bt);

    Y ~ multi_normal_cholesky(X * beta, fullGP_L(sigmasq, tausq, phi, dist, N));

}

So should I take the Cholesky decomposition of the covariance matrix (VB) in the prior of beta in "transformed data", and use multi_normal_cholesky() instead of multi_normal()?

Thanks,

Lu Zhang

