Thanks, Dan

I turned off initialization, but got the same result.

To simplify the question as far as possible:

is the code snippet below a valid way to use truncated gaussians to

draw from an asymmetric gaussian distribution, centered on y_obs[n] and with width err2 on the left and err1 on the right?

The support for y_true[n] is real<lower=0>, and all values of y_obs[n] are positive:

    for (n in 1:N)

        // Draw from either left-side Gaussian with prob f1 or right-side Gaussian with prob f2
        // Where mixing weights are

        // f1 = err2[n]/(err1[n] + err2[n])

        // f2 = err1[n]/(err1[n] + err2[n])
        mixingParam ~ uniform(0,1)
        if(mixingParam < err2[n]/(err1[n] + err2[n]) )
            // Draw from left-side Gaussian, of width err2
            y_true[n] ~ normal(y_obs[n],err2[n]) T[,y_obs[n]];
        else
            // Draw from right-side Gaussian, of width err1
            y_true[n] ~ normal(y_obs[n],err1[n]) T[y_obs[n],];

Cheers,

Joe

