Hi there!

 I'm trying to speed up my code by using vectorization, however it would involve point-wise exponentiation of the vector. From this old thread <https://groups.google.com/forum/#%21msg/stan-users/Q7zADp0Ho9E/GAp-aWInN-0J>, it seems like that is not possible and thus vectorization of this problem would not increase efficiency. I was wondering if anyone had any ideas.

My current situation is something like this (forgive the pseudocode):

data {
int N1;
int N2;

vector xvals[N2];

matrix[N1,N2] mymat
}

parameters{
real a;
real b;
...
real sigma;
}

model{
...
for (n1 in 1:N1)
   for (n2 in 1:N2)
        mymat[n1,n2] ~ normal(a*xvals[n2]^2 + b*xvals[n2]^3 + ....., sigma);
...
}

and I'd like to speed it up by doing some sort of vectorization like this:

data {
int N1;
int N2;

vector xvals[N2];

vector[N1] mymat[N2]

}

parameters{
real a;
real b;
...
real sigma;
}

model{
...
for (n1 in 1:N1)
        x[n1] ~ normal(a*xvals^2 + b*xvals^3 + ....., sigma); //This is not a valid line of code
...
}

I don't see a way around having to loop through all the elements. In a language like matlab, I could do:

x(n1,:)= myfun(a*xvals.^2 + b*xvals.^3 ....);

But this does not seem possible in Stan.

Any thoughts?

Thanks!

