From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Tue Sep 20 06:12:38 2016
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Tue, 20 Sep 2016 06:12:38 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Very different optimizing results for centered vs non centered parameterisation
Message-ID: <qt23zIqQr1Q.QVyVCI59BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <qt23zIqQr1Q.GpLAJndkBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <qt23zIqQr1Q.GpLAJndkBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/qt23zIqQr1Q/m/QVyVCI59BQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Do you have an example?  And do the centered and non-centered
parameterizations produce roughly the same posteriors for the
quantities of interest?

Optimization drops any Jacobians due to constraints.  I wrote
up a little case study:

  http://mc-stan.org/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html

But it's hard to say more without the code and example data.

And by nothing like, I take it you're comparing posterior modes
to posterior means?  Those aren't necessarily going to be anywhere
near each other (I have another case study explaining this in
the works).

- Bob

