Date: Tue, 20 Sep 2016 21:58:38 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Very different optimizing results for centered vs non centered parameterisation
Message-ID: <qt23zIqQr1Q.rsvxai2xBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <qt23zIqQr1Q.68g9BB6uBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <qt23zIqQr1Q.GpLAJndkBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <qt23zIqQr1Q.68g9BB6uBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/qt23zIqQr1Q/m/rsvxai2xBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Because sampling targets a conditional measure and optimization targets a conditional density.  They are fundamentally different objects and transform in different ways.  In particular, a MAP is not defined for a measure but rather a density in a specific parameterization, which is why we don't want to include Jacobians that change this density when we do temporary transformations behind the scenes.

