> On Sep 21, 2016, at 5:26 PM, Bistoon <bistoon....@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Dear Lawrence
>
> The number and place of knots is solved by using Penalty in B-Spline. When we use Penalty in B-Spline, we can choose a large number of knots (about 10 to 20 is recommended) to ensure the desired flexibility. In fact, the penalty control the variance of estimated parameters. If change "num_knots= 20" in attached file (for instance 15 , 30), you will see the results do not change much.
>
> Comparing GPR and Penalized B-Spline is a good idea. I will do it as soon as possible and inform you. If you have the Stan model of GPR, please send me it.
>
> - Bistoon
>
> On Wednesday, September 21, 2016 at 4:48:50 PM UTC+3:30, Mike Lawrence wrote:
> Neat! Have you compared this to a Gaussian Process? I used splines back in my frequentist days (via mgcv::gam), but it always irked me that one had to choose the the number of knots. But possibly sampling performance is far better for problems with reasonably guessable knot number?
>
> On Wednesday, 21 September 2016, Bistoon <bistoon....@gmail.com> wrote:
> Dear All,
>
> I have written a code for Penalized B-Spline implantation and attached it here. It is based on Chapter 14 in this book:"Alston, C.L., Mengersen, K.L. and Pettitt, A.N. eds., 2012. Case studies in Bayesian statistical modelling and analysis. John Wiley & Sons."
>
> Please see it and let me know your opinion. You should have already installed "rstan" and "splines" library in R. I hope to be useful to a person.
>
>
> With Best Wishes for Stan Group,
> Bistoon
