From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Thu Sep 29 21:22:26 2016
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Thu, 29 Sep 2016 21:22:26 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] How to obtain unweighted mean instead of weighted mean in hierarchical model?
Message-ID: <P57YqmM-3nM.8CiMDPTSBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <P57YqmM-3nM.X34uyk-9BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <P57YqmM-3nM.2cT09Tm4BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <P57YqmM-3nM.X34uyk-9BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/P57YqmM-3nM/m/8CiMDPTSBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Here's how to test that things are working properly.

Simulate data form the model, fit the model, and make sure
50% of the parameters are within their posterior 50% intervals.
Repeat.

If that works, your model's coded properly.

When you have

  y ~ normal(mu[group], sigma[group]);
  mu ~ normal(mu_mu, sigma_mu)

you don't expect mu_mu to be the mean of the y.  Instead,
normal(mu_mu, sigma_mu) are trying to capture the variation
in the mu[k] across groups.

The reason to have the variance components is that you don't expect
each group to have the same variability, and the variability of the
mu will play into the variability of the mu_mu estimate.

- Bob

> On Sep 29, 2016, at 10:45 AM, Henrik Singmann <sing...@gmail.com> wrote:
