Well, the stan model I'm using is fairly massive and novel, and would only obfuscate things if I posted the full thing here. Tomorrow I'll make a smaller, toy example of the aspect of the model that's causing me issues. Really, the part of the model that is currently concerning me is a mixture modeling component.

Just some points
- I do use simplex for pi when K > 2, otherwise I use real<lower=0,upper=1>
- I'm not sure I follow. For mixtures, I assume each person has a probability of belonging to cluster 1 or 2 (for k = 2, vector<lower=0,upper=1>[N] pi), pi ~ beta(alpha, beta); alpha = mean*scale, beta = (1 - mean)*scale; scale = .2; mean ~ uniform). This gives me an overall mean, and allows individuals to individually load on each cluster with some probability pi. The scale essentially inverts the beta and places greater probability on 0 or 1, thus informing the model to try 'harder' to assign people to one cluster or another.
- Stan does have the beta distribution. I've used beta several times. Hell, my model actually works, I'm just trying to extend it to include a softmax regression within it, so you can predict group membership probabilities, but also try and encourage high probability clustering.
- haven't tried simulating data /from/ it, but have fit the model *to* simulated data.

